    "subtitle": "A frequently tested list",
    "html": "<table><tr><th>Advantage</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Trade fixed expense for variable expense</td><td>Instead of investing heavily in data centers up front (CapEx), you pay only as you consume (OpEx).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. Benefit from massive economies of scale</td><td>AWS aggregates usage from hundreds of thousands of customers, so it buys and operates at lower cost and passes savings on.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Stop guessing capacity</td><td>Scale up or down as demand changes instead of predicting it and ending up with idle or insufficient capacity.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. Increase speed and agility</td><td>New resources take minutes, so experiments are cheap and teams ship faster.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. Stop spending money running data centers</td><td>Focus on customers and products, not racking servers or paying for power and cooling.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6. Go global in minutes</td><td>Deploy to Regions worldwide to give users lower latency.</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Match the keyword: \"no upfront investment\" = variable expense; \"lower per-unit price\" = economies of scale; \"deploy in another country quickly\" = go global.</div>"
