    "subtitle": "Six pillars for good design",
    "html": "<p>A set of best practices AWS uses to help you review and improve workloads. The <b>AWS Well-Architected Tool</b> in the console guides you through the review.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Focus</th><th>Key design ideas</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Operational Excellence</td><td>Running and monitoring systems, improving processes</td><td>Automate with code, make small reversible changes, learn from failures</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Security</td><td>Protecting data, systems and assets</td><td>Strong identity, traceability, encrypt data, apply security at all layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Recovering from failure and meeting demand</td><td>Test recovery, scale horizontally, automate recovery, avoid single points of failure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Performance Efficiency</td><td>Using resources efficiently as needs change</td><td>Use managed services and serverless, go global, experiment often</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost Optimization</td><td>Avoiding unnecessary cost</td><td>Pay only for what you use, measure spend, right-size, adopt consumption models</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sustainability</td><td>Reducing environmental impact</td><td>Maximize utilization, use efficient hardware and managed services</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"an\"><b>Memory trick:</b> \"Our Security Relies on People, Costs and Sustainability\" gives O-S-R-P-C-S, the pillars in the order above.</div>"
