    "id": "t7",
    "icon": "🌍",
    "title": "1.7 AWS global infrastructure",
    "subtitle": "Regions, AZs and edge locations",
    "html": "<figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"aws-img\" src=\"/images/aws-clf/aws-clf-03-region-with-three-availability-zones-and-edge-locations-serving-.svg\" alt=\"Region with three availability zones and edge locations serving users\" width=\"564\" height=\"350\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"min-width:564px;max-width:564px\"></figure>\n<h3>Region</h3><p>A physical geographic area (such as Mumbai or N. Virginia) containing multiple, isolated AZs. Data stays in the Region you choose unless you move it, which matters for compliance.</p>\n<h3>Availability Zone (AZ)</h3><p>One or more discrete data centers with redundant power, networking and connectivity, located far enough apart to avoid shared failures yet close enough for fast replication. Every Region has at least three.</p>\n<h3>Edge locations</h3><p>Sites used by CloudFront and Route 53 to cache content and answer DNS close to users. There are many more edge locations than Regions. <b>Local Zones</b> and <b>Wavelength</b> bring compute even closer to cities and 5G networks for ultra-low latency.</p>\n<h3>How to choose a Region</h3>\n<ol><li><b>Compliance and data residency:</b> laws may require data to stay in a country.</li><li><b>Latency:</b> pick the Region closest to most users.</li><li><b>Service availability:</b> newer services launch in some Regions first.</li><li><b>Price:</b> costs vary between Regions.</li></ol>\n<h3>High availability vs fault tolerance</h3>\n<p><b>High availability</b> means the system recovers quickly from failures, such as running across two AZs behind a load balancer. <b>Fault tolerance</b> means it keeps working with no interruption even when a component fails, which usually costs more.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Global</td><td>IAM, Route 53, CloudFront, AWS Organizations</td></tr><tr><td>Regional</td><td>EC2, VPC, Lambda, RDS, DynamoDB, S3 bucket data</td></tr><tr><td>AZ-bound</td><td>An EC2 instance, an EBS volume, a subnet</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Trap:</b> S3 bucket <i>names</i> are globally unique, but a bucket's data lives in one Region. EBS volumes live in one AZ only.</div>"
