    "html": "<h3>AWS Lambda</h3>\n<p>Upload code and Lambda runs it in response to events (a file landing in S3, an API call, a schedule). There are no servers to manage, it scales automatically, and you pay only for the milliseconds your code runs. Each run is limited to <b>15 minutes</b>.</p>\n<h3>Containers</h3>\n<p>A <b>container</b> packages an app with everything it needs, so it runs the same everywhere.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it is</th><th>Choose it when</th></tr><tr><td>Amazon ECS</td><td>AWS-native container orchestrator</td><td>Simple way to run Docker containers</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon EKS</td><td>Managed Kubernetes</td><td>You already use Kubernetes or want portability</td></tr><tr><td>AWS Fargate</td><td>Serverless engine for ECS and EKS</td><td>Run containers without managing servers</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon ECR</td><td>Container image registry</td><td>Store and share container images</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Other compute services</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Elastic Beanstalk</td><td>Upload code; AWS handles deployment, scaling and load balancing (PaaS)</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon Lightsail</td><td>Simple, low-cost servers and websites with predictable monthly pricing</td></tr><tr><td>AWS Batch</td><td>Runs large numbers of batch jobs, choosing the right compute automatically</td></tr><tr><td>AWS Outposts</td><td>AWS infrastructure installed in your own data center</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> \"Run code without provisioning servers\" = Lambda. \"Containers without managing servers\" = Fargate. \"Just deploy my web app\" = Elastic Beanstalk. \"Simple VPS for a small site\" = Lightsail.</div>"
