    "title": "2.3 Storage services",
    "subtitle": "Object, block and file storage",
    "html": "<h3>Three kinds of storage</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Service</th><th>Think of it as</th></tr><tr><td>Object</td><td>Amazon S3</td><td>Files stored as objects with metadata, accessed over the internet via API</td></tr><tr><td>Block</td><td>Amazon EBS</td><td>A raw disk attached to one EC2 instance, like a hard drive</td></tr><tr><td>File</td><td>Amazon EFS, FSx</td><td>A shared file system that many servers mount at once</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service)</h3>\n<p>Virtually unlimited object storage in containers called <b>buckets</b>. It is designed for <b>99.999999999% (11 nines) durability</b> by storing data redundantly across multiple AZs. Individual objects can be up to 5 TB. Use it for backups, static websites, data lakes, media and logs.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Security:</b> buckets are private by default. Control access with IAM policies, bucket policies, and Block Public Access. Encrypt at rest by default.</li><li><b>Versioning:</b> keeps old copies of objects to recover from accidental deletes.</li><li><b>Lifecycle rules:</b> automatically move or delete objects as they age.</li></ul>\n<h3>S3 storage classes</h3>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"aws-img\" src=\"/images/aws-clf/aws-clf-04-s3-storage-classes-from-hot-to-cold-with-relative-storage-cost.svg\" alt=\"S3 storage classes from hot to cold with relative storage cost\" width=\"640\" height=\"412\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"min-width:640px;max-width:640px\"><figcaption>Hot to cold: bar length shows relative storage price, shorter means cheaper per GB</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Colder classes cost less to store but charge more or take longer to retrieve. <b>Intelligent-Tiering</b> watches access and moves objects for you, ideal when the pattern is unknown. <b>One Zone-IA</b> keeps data in a single AZ, so it is cheaper but less resilient.</p>\n<h3>EBS, EFS, and others</h3>\n<ul><li><b>EBS:</b> persistent block storage for one instance, tied to <b>one AZ</b>. Back it up with snapshots (stored in S3), which can also restore into another AZ.</li><li><b>EFS:</b> an elastic, shared Linux file system that works across multiple AZs and grows automatically.</li><li><b>FSx:</b> managed specialized file systems such as Windows File Server and Lustre.</li><li><b>Storage Gateway:</b> connects on-premises systems to cloud storage (hybrid).</li><li><b>Snow Family:</b> rugged devices to move huge datasets physically when the network is too slow.</li><li><b>AWS Backup:</b> central policy-based backups across services.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Trap:</b> EBS is one AZ, EFS is multi-AZ. S3 is not a disk you attach to a server; use EBS for that.</div>"
