    "subtitle": "Connecting and protecting your resources",
    "html": "<h3>Amazon VPC</h3>\n<p>A <b>Virtual Private Cloud</b> is your own isolated network in AWS. You choose its IP range and divide it into <b>subnets</b>, each inside one AZ. A <b>public subnet</b> has a route to the internet; a <b>private subnet</b> does not.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"aws-img\" src=\"/images/aws-clf/aws-clf-05-vpc-with-public-and-private-subnets-across-two-availability-zone.svg\" alt=\"VPC with public and private subnets across two Availability Zones\" width=\"811\" height=\"516\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"min-width:640px;max-width:811px\"><figcaption>Typical design: internet-facing resources in public subnets, sensitive resources in private subnets, spread across two AZs</figcaption></figure>\n<ul><li><b>Internet gateway:</b> lets the VPC talk to the internet.</li><li><b>NAT gateway:</b> lets private resources reach out (for updates) without being reachable from outside.</li><li><b>Security groups:</b> stateful firewall at the <b>instance</b> level, allow rules only.</li><li><b>Network ACLs:</b> stateless firewall at the <b>subnet</b> level, allow and deny rules.</li><li><b>VPC peering and Transit Gateway:</b> connect VPCs; peering is not transitive.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reaching users and spreading traffic</h3>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"aws-img\" src=\"/images/aws-clf/aws-clf-06-web-architecture-users-route-53-cloudfront-load-balancer-ec2-aut.svg\" alt=\"Web architecture: users, Route 53, CloudFront, load balancer, EC2 Auto Scaling, RDS database and S3 storage\" width=\"915\" height=\"274\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"min-width:640px;max-width:915px\"><figcaption>A request flows left to right: DNS, cache, load balancing, servers, then data</figcaption></figure>\n<table><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Elastic Load Balancing</td><td>Distributes traffic across healthy targets in multiple AZs (Application LB for HTTP, Network LB for extreme performance).</td></tr><tr><td>Auto Scaling</td><td>Adds or removes EC2 instances automatically to match demand and replace unhealthy ones.</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon Route 53</td><td>Scalable DNS plus routing policies (simple, weighted, latency, failover, geolocation) and health checks.</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon CloudFront</td><td>CDN that caches content at edge locations for low latency; also helps absorb attacks.</td></tr><tr><td>AWS Global Accelerator</td><td>Routes users over the AWS global network to the best endpoint using static IPs.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Connecting to on-premises</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Option</th><th>How</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td>Site-to-Site VPN</td><td>Encrypted tunnel over the internet</td><td>Fast to set up, variable performance</td></tr><tr><td>Direct Connect</td><td>Dedicated private fiber to AWS</td><td>Consistent speed and lower latency; not encrypted by default</td></tr><tr><td>Client VPN</td><td>Remote users connect securely</td><td>Employees working remotely</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Trap:</b> Security groups are stateful (return traffic is allowed automatically) and NACLs are stateless. Direct Connect is private but not encrypted unless you add a VPN.</div>"
